// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xvi"] = {"qs": [["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Why are exchanges required to run surveillance on commodity derivative contracts?", ["To detect manipulation, abnormal price moves and position concentration", "To ensure that prices of agricultural commodities never fall below the minimum support price", "To fix daily closing prices for each contract", "To approve each client's trading strategy in advance"], 0, "Surveillance identifies unusual trading, manipulation and excessive concentration so corrective action can be taken. It does not target any particular price level or pre-approve strategies."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "Why are commodity markets typically more exposed to seasonality than equity markets?", ["Commodity exchanges are open only during the harvest months", "Commodity prices are fixed by the government outside the harvest season", "Supply of many agricultural commodities depends on sowing and harvest cycles", "Equity prices are set annually while commodity prices change monthly"], 2, "Agricultural output arrives in harvest seasons, so supply, stocks and prices follow seasonal patterns. Exchanges trade all year and prices are market-determined, not fixed by government."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A trader has a non-speculative business loss from commodity futures this year. Under the income-tax rules, this loss can generally be:", ["Set off only against speculative income of the same year and of later assessment years", "Set off against salary income in the same year", "Set off against other business income and carried forward, subject to conditions", "Neither set off nor carried forward under any circumstances"], 2, "Non-speculative business losses can be set off against other heads except salary in the same year, and unabsorbed losses carried forward against business income, subject to timely filing of returns."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "Which of the following is a key economic function of commodity futures markets?", ["Fixing minimum support prices for agricultural crops for each crop season", "Providing working-capital loans to farmers against stored crops", "Setting import duties on bullion and edible oils", "Price discovery, giving producers and users a forward reference price"], 3, "Futures markets aggregate the views of many participants and so discover expected future prices. MSP is fixed by the government, loans come from banks and duties are a fiscal decision."], ["Commodity Options", "In options on commodity futures in India, a long call that is exercised results in the holder getting:", ["A long futures position at the strike price", "Delivery of the physical commodity at the strike price immediately", "A short futures position at the prevailing market price", "Cash equal to the premium paid, returned to him by the exchange"], 0, "Exercised options on futures devolve into futures positions: the call buyer gets a long futures position at the strike price and the writer gets the matching short position."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Under SEBI's regulations, which entity's rules, bye-laws and regulations govern day-to-day trading conduct of members in commodity derivatives?", ["The recognised stock exchange on which the member trades", "The Commodity Board of the relevant crop", "The Central Warehousing Corporation", "The state agricultural produce market committee"], 0, "Members are bound by the rules, bye-laws and regulations of the recognised exchange, approved by SEBI. Commodity boards and APMCs regulate physical markets, not exchange trading."], ["Commodity Futures", "As a commodity futures contract approaches expiry, its price should move towards the spot price at the delivery centre. This process is called:", ["Contango", "Rolling", "Convergence", "Backwardation"], 2, "Near expiry the carrying period shrinks to zero, so futures and spot prices come together; this is convergence. Contango and backwardation describe the shape of the futures curve, not the expiry process."], ["Trading Mechanism", "On an electronic commodity exchange, two buy orders are at the same price. Which order is executed first?", ["The order for the larger quantity", "The order placed by a hedger rather than a speculator", "The order placed by an institutional client", "The order that was entered earlier in time"], 3, "Exchanges follow price-time priority: at the same price, the order entered first is matched first. Size, client type or purpose of the trade do not change priority."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "A clearing member fails to pay MTM losses on time. The clearing corporation is most likely to first:", ["Cancel the trades of the member's clients from the very start of the contract", "Ask the opposite counterparties to accept lower payments for that day's settlement", "Block the member's trading and close out positions using its collateral", "Suspend trading on the entire exchange for the day"], 2, "The clearing corporation acts against the defaulting member by blocking trading, closing out positions and using the member's margins and collateral, then other default resources if needed. Counterparties are still paid in full."], ["Commodity Indices", "Why might an index provider cap the weight of a single commodity in a diversified commodity index?", ["To ensure that the index always rises when inflation rises", "Because exchanges prohibit indices containing more than one energy product", "To eliminate the need for rolling futures contracts", "To prevent one commodity's price moves from dominating index behaviour"], 3, "Weight caps keep the index diversified so that a single commodity, such as crude oil, does not drive most of the index's risk. They do not remove rolling or guarantee any inflation link."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "At the year-end, a company holds open commodity futures positions that have been marked to market daily. The cumulative MTM losses already paid are:", ["Recognised in the profit and loss account for the year", "Shown as a recoverable deposit until expiry", "Ignored until the contracts are closed", "Added to the cost of inventory automatically"], 0, "MTM losses paid daily reflect the fall in fair value of open futures and are recognised in profit or loss, unless hedge accounting rules require deferral. They are not a recoverable deposit."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A copper importer must buy 50,000 kg in two months. The optimal hedge ratio is 0.8 and one futures lot is 5,000 kg. The number of lots to buy is:", ["10", "12", "8", "4"], 2, "Number of lots = hedge ratio × exposure ÷ lot size = 0.8 × 50,000 ÷ 5,000 = 8. Ten lots would be a one-to-one hedge ignoring the hedge ratio."], ["Commodity Futures", "Crude oil futures on Indian exchanges are commonly settled in cash rather than by delivery. Their final settlement price is generally linked to:", ["The average retail price of petrol in the four metro cities", "The price fixed by the oil ministry for refiners", "The settlement price of a benchmark international crude oil futures contract", "The closing price of the near-month Indian contract on the first trading day"], 2, "Cash-settled energy contracts in India typically settle against a reference price derived from an international benchmark contract, converted to rupees. Retail fuel prices and ministry prices are not used."], ["Trading Mechanism", "The unique client code (UCC) used in commodity derivatives trading mainly helps to:", ["Fix the brokerage rate payable by each client to the member", "Decide which accredited warehouse a client must use for any delivery", "Exempt small clients from margin requirements", "Track each client's trades and positions for surveillance"], 3, "The UCC links every order to a specific client, allowing exchanges to monitor client-level positions, enforce limits and detect manipulation. It has no role in brokerage or warehouse allocation."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "Disputes between a client and a trading member that are not settled through grievance redressal can be referred under SEBI's current framework to:", ["The local civil court only, as arbitration is not available", "Online conciliation and arbitration through the ODR platform", "The Warehousing Development and Regulatory Authority", "The commodity board for the relevant crop"], 1, "SEBI's online dispute resolution framework allows unresolved disputes with market intermediaries to go for online conciliation and arbitration. WDRA and commodity boards do not resolve broker disputes."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A stockist holding chana inventory writes call options on chana futures against it. This strategy, which earns premium but caps upside, is a:", ["Covered call", "Protective put", "Long strangle", "Bear put spread"], 0, "Writing calls against an existing long position is a covered call: the premium cushions small falls but gains above the strike are given up. A protective put would buy a put instead."], ["Trading Mechanism", "When a commodity futures contract hits its daily price limit, exchanges typically:", ["Cancel all trades executed during the day and refund all margins collected to the members", "Close the contract permanently and settle at the previous day's price", "Permit trades only within the band, relaxing it in phases after a cooling-off", "Allow unlimited price movement for the rest of the day"], 2, "Daily price limits restrict trades beyond the band. Exchanges usually provide for a cooling-off period and phased relaxation of the limit, rather than cancelling trades or delisting the contract."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Brokerage and exchange transaction charges paid by a business trader in commodity derivatives are generally:", ["Added to Commodity Transaction Tax payable", "Not deductible because they relate to derivatives", "Deductible only against capital gains", "Deductible as business expenses in computing business income"], 3, "For a trader whose derivatives income is business income, brokerage and exchange charges are ordinary deductible expenses. There is no capital gains head involved in such business income."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "After the FMC–SEBI merger, the Forward Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1952 was:", ["Retained as the main law with SEBI as its administrator", "Repealed, with commodity derivatives brought under the SCRA framework", "Amended to apply only to agricultural commodities", "Merged into the Warehousing (Development and Regulation) Act"], 1, "The Finance Act, 2015 repealed the FCRA and brought commodity derivatives within the definition of securities under the SCRA, with SEBI as regulator. The warehousing law is separate."], ["Commodity Indices", "The three components of the total return of a fully collateralised commodity futures index are:", ["Dividend yield, roll yield and currency return", "Spot return, storage cost and insurance cost", "Coupon return, roll yield and inflation return", "Spot return, roll yield and interest on collateral"], 3, "A fully collateralised index earns from spot price changes, from rolling contracts along the curve, and from interest on the cash backing the futures. Commodities pay no dividends or coupons."], ["Commodity Options", "Using put-call parity for European options on futures, if the call and put on the same strike have equal premiums, the futures price is most likely:", ["Well above the strike price", "Equal to the strike price", "Well below the strike price", "Equal to zero, as parity has broken down"], 1, "Put-call parity for options on futures gives C − P = (F − K) discounted. If C equals P, then F equals K, so the futures price is at the strike."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A client calls his broker to place an order but does not specify the price. The broker places a market order. A market order:", ["Has execution certainty but no price certainty", "Has price certainty but no execution certainty", "Is executed only at the previous day's settlement price", "Can be modified by the exchange to the best bid of the next day"], 0, "A market order is executed immediately against the best available counter-orders, so the trade is almost certain but the price is not known in advance. A limit order provides price certainty instead."], ["Commodity Options", "A trader buys a put option on a commodity with a strike of ₹900 at a premium of ₹25. The lot is 100 units. If the underlying settles at ₹850 at expiry, his net profit is:", ["₹5,000", "₹7,500", "₹2,500", "₹2,000"], 2, "Intrinsic value at expiry = 900 − 850 = ₹50. Net profit per unit = 50 − 25 = ₹25, so for 100 units profit = ₹2,500. ₹5,000 ignores the premium paid."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A manufacturer takes delivery of copper through a commodity exchange contract. On the physical delivery, the manufacturer will typically pay:", ["Commodity Transaction Tax only, without GST", "Goods and Services Tax on the value of the goods delivered", "Securities Transaction Tax on the delivery value", "No tax, as exchange deliveries are tax exempt"], 1, "Physical delivery is a supply of goods, so GST applies on its value. CTT relates to the derivative transaction itself, and STT applies only to securities like shares."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "An authorised person (AP) of a broker collects margin money from a client in cash in his own name. This is:", ["Permitted if the AP issues a handwritten receipt", "Permitted for amounts below the daily margin requirement of that particular client account", "Required to speed up margin payments", "Not permitted, as client funds must reach the broker through banking channels"], 3, "Authorised persons cannot accept client funds in their own name; payments must go to the broker's designated client bank account. Cash dealings in an AP's name create risk of misuse."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "A dealer uses his knowledge of a client's large pending sell order to sell for his own account first. This is a breach mainly because it:", ["Breaches only the exchange's dress code for dealers", "Puts the dealer's interest ahead of the client's: front running", "Is permitted if the dealer later informs the client about the trade and its price", "Is acceptable when the market is volatile"], 1, "Trading ahead of a client's order using knowledge of that order is front running, a breach of the duty to act in the client's interest. Later disclosure does not cure it."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "A jeweller in Jaipur buys gold in the spot market and simultaneously sells gold futures when futures trade well above the full cost of carry. The jeweller is acting as a/an:", ["Speculator, betting that gold prices will rise sharply over the coming months", "Arbitrageur, locking in the gap over carrying cost", "Hedger of a future purchase requirement", "Market maker earning only the bid-ask spread"], 1, "Buying spot and selling overpriced futures (cash-and-carry) locks in a near riskless gain if futures exceed spot plus carrying costs. It is an arbitrage, not a directional bet or a purchase hedge."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Initial margin paid to a broker for commodity futures is generally shown in the trader's books as:", ["An expense in the profit and loss account", "A current asset, as a deposit recoverable on closure of positions", "Income received in advance", "Part of the cost of the commodity purchased"], 1, "Initial margin is a deposit, not an expense, so it appears as a current asset until positions are closed. Only realised and MTM gains or losses affect profit."], ["Commodity Indices", "A commodity futures index has consistently underperformed the spot price of its main constituent over several years. The most likely reason is that the futures curve has been in:", ["Backwardation, causing positive roll yield", "Perfect convergence with zero basis", "Inverted spread with a high convenience yield", "Contango, causing negative roll yield"], 3, "When futures are in contango, each roll buys a dearer contract, so index returns lag the spot price. Backwardation would have the opposite effect and add to returns."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "Compared with an over-the-counter forward contract, an exchange-traded commodity futures contract mainly removes which risk for the two parties?", ["Price risk, because futures prices cannot move against either party", "Basis risk, because futures always converge to the local mandi price at expiry", "Liquidity risk in the underlying physical commodity market", "Counterparty default risk, as the clearing corporation guarantees settlement"], 3, "The clearing corporation becomes the counterparty to every trade and collects margins, so default risk is largely removed. Price risk remains with the position holder, and basis risk still exists between futures and local spot prices."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "Segregation of client funds by a commodity broker means that:", ["Each client's trades are executed on different exchanges", "Client money is kept separately and cannot be used for the broker's own trading", "Clients must keep their funds in a separate bank chosen by the exchange", "Clients' profits are held by the broker until the year-end"], 1, "Client funds must be maintained separately from the broker's own funds and used only for that client's obligations. Using client money for proprietary trading is a serious violation."], ["Commodity Indices", "A spot-price commodity index differs from a futures-based index in that a spot index:", ["Includes roll yield and interest on collateral in its reported returns", "Is the only type of index permitted to have index futures in India", "Is always calculated only once at the end of each month", "Tracks physical prices and is hard for an investor to replicate"], 3, "A spot index tracks current physical prices, which an investor cannot easily hold because of storage and delivery issues. Futures-based indices are designed to be investable and include roll and collateral returns."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "An electronic negotiable warehouse receipt (e-NWR) is useful to a farmer mainly because it:", ["Guarantees that the produce will be sold at the minimum support price announced by government", "Can be pledged for finance or transferred without moving the produce", "Exempts the stored produce from quality testing by the warehouse", "Converts the produce automatically into an open futures position"], 1, "An e-NWR is a transferable electronic title to goods stored in a registered warehouse, so it can be used as collateral or traded. It does not guarantee any price and stored goods are still graded."], ["Commodity Options", "A gold call option has a strike of ₹71,000 while the underlying futures trade at ₹72,500 and the premium is ₹1,900. The time value of the option is:", ["₹1,500", "₹1,900", "₹3,400", "₹400"], 3, "Intrinsic value = 72,500 − 71,000 = ₹1,500. Time value = premium − intrinsic value = 1,900 − 1,500 = ₹400."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "A stock broker wishes to offer commodity derivatives trading to clients. Under current SEBI rules it must:", ["Obtain a separate licence from the Ministry of Consumer Affairs for each commodity it wishes to offer", "Register with the WDRA as a warehouse service provider", "Be a SEBI-registered stock broker with membership of the exchange's commodity segment", "Only obtain approval from its bank for handling client funds"], 2, "Commodity derivatives brokers are SEBI-registered stock brokers with membership of the relevant segment of a recognised exchange. No separate ministry or WDRA licence is required for broking."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A refiner fears that the margin between crude oil prices and product prices will narrow. A position in crude oil futures against offsetting positions in petroleum product futures is known as a:", ["Crush spread", "Calendar spread", "Crack spread", "Butterfly spread"], 2, "The crack spread captures the refining margin between crude and its products. The crush spread relates soybeans to meal and oil, while a calendar spread uses different expiries of the same commodity."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "A client discovers trades in his account that he never authorised. His first step should be to:", ["Complain to the broker at once and escalate to the exchange or SEBI if unresolved", "Wait for the year-end statement of accounts before taking any action against the broker", "Settle the losses directly with the opposite counterparty", "Close his bank account to stop margin payments"], 0, "Unauthorised trades should be disputed promptly with the broker in writing; unresolved complaints can then be escalated to the exchange, SEBI's SCORES and online dispute resolution. Delay weakens the client's case."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "In a compulsory-delivery agricultural contract, a seller wishing to deliver must ensure that the commodity:", ["Is transported directly to the buyer's factory by the seller at the seller's own cost and risk", "Is sold first in the local mandi and the sale proceeds deposited with the exchange", "Is in an accredited warehouse and meets the contract's quality specifications", "Is certified only by the seller's own quality inspector"], 2, "Exchange deliveries take place through accredited warehouses, and the goods must be tested and certified by approved assayers as meeting contract specifications. Self-certification is not accepted."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "Through novation, the clearing corporation in a commodity futures trade:", ["Acts only as a record keeper without taking any counterparty role", "Transfers the trade to a warehouse for delivery on the same day", "Guarantees a profit to both the buyer and the seller", "Becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer"], 3, "Novation substitutes the clearing corporation as counterparty to both sides, which is the basis of its settlement guarantee. It does not guarantee profits and is not merely a record keeper."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "Copper, zinc, aluminium and nickel are grouped in commodity markets as:", ["Bullion", "Energy commodities", "Base metals", "Soft commodities"], 2, "Industrial non-ferrous metals such as copper, zinc, aluminium and nickel are called base metals. Bullion refers to precious metals like gold and silver, and soft commodities are agricultural products."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A trader buys near-month cardamom futures and sells far-month cardamom futures, expecting the price gap to narrow. This is an example of a/an:", ["Inter-commodity spread", "Inter-exchange arbitrage", "Outright directional position", "Calendar (intra-commodity) spread"], 3, "Positions in the same commodity across different expiries form a calendar or intra-commodity spread, which profits from changes in the price difference rather than the overall price level."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "Which of the following must a trading member provide to clients periodically under the regulatory framework?", ["A statement of accounts for funds and positions", "A guarantee of minimum annual return", "A copy of the broker's internal audit report", "The names of other clients trading the same commodity"], 0, "Brokers must send periodic statements of funds and securities or positions, along with contract notes. Guaranteeing returns or sharing other clients' details would be improper."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "A clearing corporation increases margins on a contract when it enters the delivery period mainly because:", ["Exchanges earn more fees from higher margins", "The risk of delivery default and price manipulation rises close to expiry", "Delivery contracts are not covered by the Settlement Guarantee Fund", "Speculators are legally barred from holding positions in the delivery period"], 1, "Near expiry, open positions turn into delivery obligations, increasing the risk of default and squeeze. Higher delivery-period margins protect the system. Margins are not a fee income."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Before trading in commodity derivatives, a client receives the 'Risk Disclosure Document'. Its main purpose is to:", ["Guarantee a minimum return to clients who follow the broker's research advice", "Record the client's bank account details for margin payments", "Exempt the broker from any liability for errors in execution", "Explain the risks of leveraged trading so the client decides knowingly"], 3, "The risk disclosure document informs clients about leverage, volatility, liquidity and other risks of derivatives trading. It gives no guarantee and does not exempt brokers from their obligations."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Why is trading in exchange-traded commodity derivatives, on which CTT is paid, treated as non-speculative for income-tax purposes?", ["Tax law excludes eligible exchange trades from speculative transactions", "Because all commodity contracts on Indian exchanges are settled by compulsory physical delivery", "Because commodity profits are fully exempt from tax", "Because CTT replaces income tax on such profits"], 0, "The tax law carves out eligible commodity derivative trades on recognised exchanges from speculative transactions, so they are non-speculative business income. CTT is an additional transaction tax, not a substitute for income tax."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "The Investor Protection Fund of an exchange is primarily used to:", ["Pay compensation for trading losses due to adverse price moves", "Compensate eligible clients when a member is declared a defaulter", "Fund the exchange's advertising and marketing", "Pay CTT on behalf of small clients"], 1, "The IPF compensates genuine claims of clients against defaulting members, subject to limits, and supports investor awareness. Market losses from price moves are not compensated."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "Which margin is designed to cover losses in situations beyond those captured by the normal VaR-based initial margin?", ["Mark-to-market margin", "Pay-in margin", "Extreme loss margin", "Delivery period margin"], 2, "The extreme loss margin is an additional buffer for price moves larger than those assumed in the VaR or SPAN-based initial margin. MTM covers realised daily losses, not tail risk."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "Mark-to-market settlement in commodity futures means that:", ["Profits are paid only when the position is finally closed or the contract expires", "Margins are fixed once at the start of the contract and are never revised by the exchange afterwards", "Only losses are collected while profits are held back until expiry", "Open positions are revalued at the daily settlement price and gains or losses settled daily"], 3, "MTM settles notional gains and losses daily against the settlement price, preventing losses from accumulating. Profits are credited and losses debited each day."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "Which statement about participants in Indian commodity derivatives markets is correct as per the current framework?", ["Eligible FPIs may trade non-agricultural commodity derivatives, subject to SEBI conditions", "Only farmers and farmer producer organisations may trade agricultural futures on exchanges", "Mutual funds are prohibited from taking any exposure at all to commodity derivatives", "Retail individuals may trade only options and not futures"], 0, "SEBI has opened commodity derivatives to institutional participants such as eligible FPIs (in non-agricultural contracts), mutual funds and PMS within limits. Retail investors may trade both futures and options."], ["Commodity Indices", "An Indian exchange launches futures on a bullion index. Compared with buying separate gold and silver futures, a trader using the index future mainly gains:", ["Physical delivery of gold and silver in fixed proportions", "Protection from all price falls in gold and silver", "Exemption from margin requirements", "A single position giving diversified exposure to the bullion basket"], 3, "Index futures offer exposure to a basket through one contract. They are cash-settled, so no physical delivery takes place, and they carry normal price risk and margin requirements."], ["Trading Mechanism", "An immediate-or-cancel (IOC) order on a commodity exchange:", ["Remains in the order book until the end of the trading day", "Is executed only at the closing session price", "Must be fully executed or else it is converted to a market order", "Executes whatever quantity is available at once and cancels the rest"], 3, "An IOC order is matched immediately to the extent possible and any unexecuted quantity is cancelled. A day order would remain in the book until the session ends."], ["Commodity Futures", "A commodity's contract specification says 'tick size ₹1 per quintal, trading unit 10 MT'. The value of a one-tick move per lot is:", ["₹100", "₹10", "₹1,000", "₹1"], 0, "10 MT equals 100 quintals. A ₹1 per quintal move therefore changes the lot value by 100 × ₹1 = ₹100. Using 10 or 1,000 confuses the units."], ["Commodity Options", "Other things remaining the same, an increase in the expected volatility of the underlying commodity will:", ["Increase call premiums but reduce put premiums", "Reduce the premiums of both calls and puts", "Increase the premiums of both calls and puts", "Have no effect, as volatility affects only futures prices"], 2, "Higher volatility raises the chance of large favourable moves for both calls and puts, so both premiums rise. The holder's loss is limited to the premium, so the extra volatility is valuable to both."], ["Commodity Futures", "A trader buys 3 lots of a commodity future, each of 1,000 units, at ₹215.50. The day's settlement price is ₹212.80. His mark-to-market result is:", ["Loss of ₹8,100", "Profit of ₹8,100", "Loss of ₹2,700", "Loss of ₹81,000"], 0, "Change per unit = 212.80 − 215.50 = −₹2.70. For a long position of 3 × 1,000 units, loss = 2.70 × 3,000 = ₹8,100. ₹2,700 would be the loss on a single lot."], ["Commodity Futures", "A spot price of ₹60,000 and a three-month futures price of ₹60,900 imply an annualised cost of carry (simple) of about:", ["6%", "1.5%", "9%", "4.5%"], 0, "The three-month premium is 900 ÷ 60,000 = 1.5%. Annualised, 1.5% × 4 = 6%. 1.5% is the unannualised figure, a common mistake."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Which body regulates the warehouses that issue negotiable warehouse receipts in India?", ["Securities and Exchange Board of India", "Warehousing Development and Regulatory Authority", "Reserve Bank of India", "Food Corporation of India"], 1, "The WDRA regulates warehouses under the Warehousing (Development and Regulation) Act, 2007, including registration and e-NWRs. SEBI regulates exchanges and intermediaries, while FCI is a procurement agency."], ["Commodity Futures", "In a 'both-option' delivery contract, which statement is correct about positions open at expiry?", ["Every open position must be settled by physical delivery at the delivery centre without any exception", "All open positions are closed out at the international reference price", "Delivery occurs only where the seller's and buyer's delivery intentions are matched", "Only the buyer can choose whether delivery will take place"], 2, "In both-option contracts, delivery happens when the seller's intention to deliver is matched with a buyer's intention to take delivery; unmatched positions are cash settled. Compulsory delivery contracts require delivery of all open positions."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A farmer holding a short futures hedge will be better off than expected when the basis (spot minus futures):", ["Weakens, i.e. becomes more negative", "Strengthens, i.e. becomes more positive", "Remains exactly unchanged", "Becomes equal to the cost of carry"], 1, "A short hedger is long the physical and short futures, so he gains if spot rises relative to futures, which is a strengthening basis. A weakening basis hurts the short hedger."], ["Commodity Options", "Time decay (theta) generally works in favour of:", ["Option buyers", "Option writers", "Holders of long futures positions", "Both option buyers and writers equally"], 1, "As time passes, the time value of options erodes. Writers have received the premium and gain as time value falls, while buyers lose. Futures positions have no time value."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "SEBI may suspend trading in a particular agricultural commodity derivative. The most likely reason is:", ["The commodity having too many registered warehouses in the producing states", "A fall in trading volumes on the exchange for one day", "A request from a single large trader holding a losing position in that contract", "Concerns about excessive volatility or its effect on food inflation"], 3, "Regulators have occasionally suspended trading in sensitive agricultural contracts amid inflation or volatility concerns. A single trader's losses or warehouse numbers are not grounds for suspension."], ["Commodity Options", "An option writer who has sold a call is most concerned about which Greek increasing sharply as expiry approaches for an at-the-money option?", ["Gamma", "Rho", "Vega of a far-month option", "Lambda of the futures"], 0, "Gamma, the rate of change of delta, is highest for at-the-money options near expiry, so the writer's exposure can swing rapidly. Rho measures interest-rate sensitivity and is usually minor."], ["Codes of Conduct and Investor Protection", "A broker's dealer tells a new client that a particular commodity strategy 'can never make a loss'. This is:", ["Misrepresentation, which the code of conduct prohibits", "Acceptable if the client signs the risk disclosure document", "Permitted for hedging strategies only", "Acceptable marketing as long as no fee is charged"], 0, "Guaranteeing returns or claiming a derivatives strategy is loss-free is misleading. The code of conduct requires fair and accurate representation of risks, which a signed risk disclosure does not override."], ["Commodity Indices", "In a commodity futures index, the return earned from replacing an expiring contract with a cheaper later contract when the market is in backwardation is called:", ["Collateral yield", "Spot return", "Positive roll yield", "Convenience premium"], 2, "In backwardation, later contracts trade below the expiring one, so rolling means selling high and buying lower, producing a positive roll yield. Spot return comes from changes in spot prices and collateral yield from interest on margin money."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "On which of the following commodity derivative transactions is Commodity Transaction Tax generally NOT payable?", ["Derivatives on agricultural commodities notified as exempt", "Futures on gold traded on a recognised stock exchange in India", "Options on crude oil futures", "Futures on base metals such as copper"], 0, "CTT applies to non-agricultural commodity derivatives; derivatives on specified agricultural commodities are exempt. Gold, crude oil and base metal contracts attract CTT."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A trader wants to buy only if the price rises to ₹9,150 so as to limit losses on an existing short position. Which order suits this need?", ["A sell limit order at ₹9,150", "A buy stop-loss order with a trigger price of ₹9,150", "A buy limit order at ₹8,800", "An immediate-or-cancel sell order at the market price"], 1, "A stop-loss buy order becomes active only when the trigger price is reached, so it caps losses on a short position. A buy limit order at a lower price would not protect against a rise."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A trader buys a ₹6,000 strike call at ₹180 and sells a ₹6,200 strike call at ₹90 on the same commodity and expiry. His maximum profit per unit is:", ["₹110", "₹200", "₹90", "₹290"], 0, "This bull call spread costs a net ₹90 (180 − 90). The maximum pay-off is the strike difference of ₹200, so maximum profit = 200 − 90 = ₹110. The maximum loss is the ₹90 net premium."], ["Commodity Indices", "An index that weights constituents in proportion to the value of their world production is said to use:", ["Equal weighting", "Liquidity-based weighting", "Price-weighting by contract size", "Production-based weighting"], 3, "Production-value weighting reflects each commodity's economic importance. Liquidity-based weighting uses trading volumes instead, and equal weighting gives each constituent the same share."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "The Settlement Guarantee Fund (Core SGF) maintained by a clearing corporation is used to:", ["Pay daily mark-to-market profits to all clients", "Fund the exchange's technology and staff costs", "Meet settlement obligations if a clearing member defaults", "Compensate traders for losses due to price movements"], 2, "The core SGF is a default fund that allows the clearing corporation to complete settlement when a member fails. Normal MTM pay-outs come from losing members, and market losses are not compensated."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Client-level position limits in an agricultural commodity are usually linked to:", ["The estimated deliverable supply or production of that commodity", "The client's annual income as declared in the KYC form", "The broker's net worth only", "The number of trading days left in the contract"], 0, "For agricultural commodities, position limits are generally set with reference to the deliverable supply so that no single participant can corner the market. The client's income is relevant for suitability, not exchange limits."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Recognised commodity derivatives exchanges in India must have a separate clearing corporation mainly to:", ["Allow the exchange to avoid SEBI regulation of its trading platform", "Enable the exchange to own and operate warehouses directly", "Separate trading from clearing and strengthen risk management", "Provide loans to clients for margin payments when prices move sharply"], 2, "Separating clearing into a distinct entity improves risk management and governance. Clearing corporations are themselves regulated by SEBI and do not provide margin loans."], ["Commodity Indices", "A commodity index is rebalanced annually to its target weights. After a year in which crude oil rose sharply and grains fell, rebalancing will typically:", ["Add further to crude oil and reduce grains", "Leave weights unchanged, as rebalancing affects only roll dates", "Reduce the weight of crude oil and add to grains", "Remove grains from the index permanently"], 2, "Rebalancing restores target weights, so outperformers like crude, now overweight, are trimmed and underperformers, now underweight, are topped up. Constituents are not removed merely because of one year's fall."], ["Commodity Futures", "Which factor would cause the futures price of a storable commodity to trade BELOW its spot price?", ["An increase in interest rates", "A rise in warehousing charges", "An increase in insurance costs on stored goods", "A high convenience yield because current stocks are scarce"], 3, "When inventories are tight, holders value physical stock highly and the convenience yield can outweigh carrying costs, pushing futures below spot (backwardation). Higher interest, storage or insurance raise the futures price."], ["Commodity Futures", "A commodity trades at ₹4,800 per quintal in the spot market. With a 9% annual (simple) financing rate and storage charges of ₹20 per quintal for two months, what should a two-month futures contract be worth?", ["₹4,892", "₹4,872", "₹4,820", "₹4,932"], 0, "Interest for two months is 4,800 × 9% × 2/12 = ₹72. Fair price = 4,800 + 72 + 20 = ₹4,892. ₹4,872 ignores storage cost, and ₹4,820 ignores interest."], ["Introduction to Commodity Markets", "A trader buys silver futures purely because he expects prices to rise over the next week and has no exposure to physical silver. He is best described as a:", ["Hedger", "Speculator", "Arbitrageur", "Market maker"], 1, "A speculator takes a position to profit from expected price moves without an underlying physical exposure. A hedger offsets an existing risk, while an arbitrageur exploits price differences between markets."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A cotton mill buys cotton futures at ₹5,200 to hedge a future purchase. When it buys cotton in the spot market at ₹5,600, it closes the futures at ₹5,550. Its effective purchase cost is:", ["₹5,250", "₹5,600", "₹5,200", "₹5,950"], 0, "Futures gain = 5,550 − 5,200 = ₹350. Effective cost = spot price − futures gain = 5,600 − 350 = ₹5,250. The difference from ₹5,200 reflects the change in basis."], ["Strategies using Commodity Futures and Options", "A trader buys a gold call and a gold put, both with strike ₹72,000, paying ₹1,100 and ₹900 respectively. His break-even prices at expiry are:", ["₹71,100 and ₹72,900", "₹70,000 and ₹74,000", "₹70,900 and ₹73,100", "₹72,000 only"], 1, "A long straddle costs 1,100 + 900 = ₹2,000. It breaks even when the price moves ₹2,000 either side of the strike: ₹70,000 and ₹74,000."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "An exporter uses commodity futures solely to hedge the price of goods it will sell. For accounting purposes, it should:", ["Treat the futures as long-term investments carried at historical cost until they mature", "Document the hedge relationship and apply hedge accounting if eligible", "Ignore the futures until the export sale is completed", "Record the full contract value as a liability"], 1, "Hedge accounting requires documentation of the hedged item, the hedging instrument and effectiveness. Without it, futures are measured at fair value through profit or loss. Contract value is not recorded as a liability."], ["Commodity Options", "A put option on crude oil futures has a strike of ₹6,500 and the futures are trading at ₹6,700. The put is:", ["In the money", "Out of the money", "At the money", "Deep in the money"], 1, "A put has intrinsic value only when the underlying is below the strike. With futures at ₹6,700 above the ₹6,500 strike, the put is out of the money."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "A person repeatedly places and cancels large orders without the intention of trading, to create a false impression of demand. This practice is:", ["Legitimate hedging", "Spoofing, a prohibited manipulative practice", "Permitted arbitrage", "Normal market making"], 1, "Placing orders without intent to execute, to mislead other participants, is spoofing, which is prohibited under SEBI's rules on fraudulent and unfair trade practices."], ["Clearing, Settlement and Risk Management", "A trader buys one gold mini contract of 100 grams at ₹72,000 per 10 grams. If the initial margin is 8%, the initial margin required is:", ["₹5,760", "₹72,000", "₹57,600", "₹7,20,000"], 2, "Contract value = (100 ÷ 10) × 72,000 = ₹7,20,000. Initial margin at 8% = ₹57,600. ₹7,20,000 is the full contract value, not the margin."]]};
